! Pressure stabilization against the MINI element it replaces, on quadrilaterals.
!
! One of five sifs in this directory, one per element family, all the same
! problem: a unit square held on one face and sheared on the opposite one.
! Only the mesh differs.
!
! WHAT THE SET PINS. The stabilization constant is per element family and
! MEASURED, not derived, and it spans a factor of fourteen: 0.0417 for a
! quadrilateral down to 0.0029 for a tetrahedron. That spread is the whole
! reason the constants are stated per family, so every family needs a case --
! a single one would leave four constants unguarded. The constants are chosen
! so that the default coefficient of one reproduces MINI, which is what the
! probe below checks.
!
! Both discretisations run here as two solvers on one problem. That is possible
! because this case is elastic: with "Maxwell Material" there is a "ve_stress"
! integration point history exported under a fixed name, which two solvers would
! share and corrupt. The creep counterpart therefore lives apart, in
! ViscoElasticMaxwellStabilized.
!
! The two NORMS are not comparable with each other -- MINI carries bubble
! degrees of freedom in the global system -- so each pins its own leg. The
! SaveScalars probe is what compares them, and it is a physical quantity:
!
!            d 1 at the probe
!   MINI     1.227186
!   stab     1.227324      +0.011 %
!
! at the default coefficient of one, which is what the family constant is
! calibrated to give.

Header
  Mesh DB "." "square"
End

Simulation
  Max Output Level = 5
  Coordinate System = Cartesian 2D
  Simulation Type = Steady State
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
End

Body 1
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
End

! No Poisson ratio: "Incompressible" sets the Lame parameters itself, lambda to
! zero and mu to E/3, and ignores whatever ratio is given. Stating one here
! would only imply it mattered -- verified by running at 0.4999, 0.45 and 0.3
! and getting identical norms to every digit.
Material 1
  Density = Real 0
  Youngs Modulus = Real 1
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers(4) = 1 2 3 4
End

! The bubble count is not free: getBubbleDOFs offers 1, 3, 6 ... on triangles
! and quadrilaterals and 1, 4, 10 ... on tetrahedra, prisms and bricks, and
! asking for a number in between does NOT round up -- BDOFs takes the number
! asked for and the assembly uses that many of the functions available, which
! is a partial enrichment rather than a coarser element. Here b:3, because
! a quadrilateral needs more than one: Q1 plus a single bubble is not a
! stable pair, and every other 2D case in the tree uses three.
Solver 1
  Equation = "Elasticity MINI"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = String "m[md:2 mp:1]"
  Element = p:1 b:3
  Incompressible = Logical True
  Large Deflection = False
  Displace Mesh = Logical False
  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-9
End

Solver 2
  Equation = "Elasticity Stabilized"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = String "s[sd:2 sp:1]"
  ! No "Element" declaration, deliberately. Writing "p:1" here bought nothing --
  ! at degree one there are no edge, face or bubble functions for the p-version to
  ! add -- but it made the element an ACTIVE P-ELEMENT, and that is not free. A
  ! p-element carries PDefs, whose GaussPoints field is set mesh-wide by
  ! SetMeshMaxDOFs from the largest demand any solver places on those elements.
  ! Sharing a mesh with the MINI leg and its "p:1 b:N" therefore handed this
  ! solver the bubble's integration rule: 150 points on a tetrahedron where 4 is
  ! the element's own and 1 is sufficient. A plain nodal element never consults
  ! PDefs and simply takes its rule from elements.def.
  Incompressible = Logical True
  Large Deflection = False
  Displace Mesh = Logical False
  Pressure Stabilization = Logical True
  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-9
End

Solver 3
  Equation = "SaveProbe"
  Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
  Exec Solver = After Simulation
  Show Norm Index = 10
  Save Coordinates(1,2) = 0.5 1.0
End

! The third leg: Taylor-Hood, P2 displacement over P1 pressure. The pressure
! elimination at the end of LocalMatrix clears every non-nodal pressure dof, so
! "Element = p:2" IS the P2/P1 pair -- no bubble, no stabilization. It is the
! inf-sup stable pair the other two legs approximate by different means, which
! makes it the natural third point of comparison here.
!
! No "Serendipity P Elements" declaration: the default (serendipity) is what a
! user gets, so that is what is tested. On this family it costs 0.2% in the
! probe displacement against the full basis -- measured, not assumed -- while
! the SOLUTION NORM moves 17%, for the dof-counting reason set out below.
Solver 4
  Equation = "Elasticity Taylor-Hood"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = String "t[td:2 tp:1]"
  Element = p:2
  Element Integration Points = "-quad 9"
  Incompressible = Logical True
  Large Deflection = False
  Displace Mesh = Logical False
  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-9
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Target Boundaries(1) = 1
  md 1 = Real 0
  md 2 = Real 0
  sd 1 = Real 0
  sd 2 = Real 0
  td 1 = Real 0
  td 2 = Real 0
End

Boundary Condition 2
  Target Boundaries(1) = 3
  Force 1 = Real 0.1
End

! ONLY THE STABILIZED LEG CARRIES A NORM. The MINI leg is here as the reference
! the probe is compared against, and its norm is deliberately not pinned: a
! solution vector norm counts the bubble and pressure degrees of freedom along
! with the displacement, and those are not well determined even where the
! displacement is. It showed: on macOS, ARM and Windows the MINI norm came back
! 2.40 against the 0.104 measured on x86 Linux, while the DISPLACEMENT at the
! probe was fine -- it holds to four digits across bubble counts b:1 through b:4,
! which an ill-posed problem would not. The stabilized norm, by contrast,
! reproduced to 2.5e-10 on every platform. So the reproducible quantity is
! pinned and the fragile one is not.
!
! The tolerance below is still loosened from the default 1e-5, and guards what
! this test is
! for -- doubling this family's stabilization constant moves the norm by
! 7.9e-3, so there is a factor of about 7 of margin.
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1.0e-3
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm = Real 0.69240337E-01

! THE TAYLOR-HOOD LEG IS PINNED BY THE PROBE DISPLACEMENT, NOT BY ITS NORM, and
! for exactly the reason given above for MINI: p:2 carries edge dofs, its
! solution vector norm counts them along with the pressure, and a norm over dofs
! that are not themselves well determined is not a quantity to pin. The
! displacement at the probe is, so that is what is checked. Column 10 is "td 1"
! -- two components plus a pressure per leg, after the three coordinates.
Solver 3 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.230491764348E-001
